From: DKR-R Win95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Publish the DP edge before rendering, not after

DKR crashes in __scHandleRDP with sc->curRDPTask null. Twelve hypotheses about
ordering were eliminated one by one -- duplicate edges, SP/DP races, queue
saturation, wrong target task -- and every one of them was wrong, because the
defect is not an ordering defect at all.

DKR's scheduler carries a watchdog that stock libultra does not have. In
__scHandleRetrace (libultra/src/sc/sched.c):

    if (sc->curRDPTask) gCurRDPTaskCounter++;

    if ((gCurRDPTaskCounter > 10) && (sc->curRDPTask)) {
        set_curRDPTask_NULL = TRUE;
        osDpSetStatus(... DPC_CLR_PIPE_CTR | DPC_CLR_CMD_CTR);
    }
    ...
    if (set_curRDPTask_NULL) sc->curRDPTask = NULL;

Past ten retraces with an outstanding RDP task, Rare's code declares the RDP
hung: it nulls curRDPTask and resets the DP registers, but it does NOT clear
OS_SC_NEEDS_RDP on the task. A DP edge arriving after that walks into
__scHandleRDP, reads curRDPTask == 0 and dereferences null.

That is exactly the state the probe captured at the instant of reception:

    msg=668 curRSP=0x00000000 curRDP=0x00000000 <== NUL
    visee=0x80125FB0 state=0x00000001 flags=0x00000023

curRDPTask null while the targeted task still carries NEEDS_RDP (state = 1).
Only two code paths null curRDPTask, and __scHandleRDP clears NEEDS_RDP when it
does. The watchdog is the only path that leaves this combination behind.

Ten retraces is 167 ms. The DP edge was published after send_dl, and a software
Glide frame on an emulated Pentium II exceeds that budget, so the watchdog was
guaranteed to fire eventually -- no ordering fix could have helped.

Publishing the edge before rendering is safe for exactly the reason the SP edge
already is: the queued task owns an immutable RDRAM snapshot, so the renderer
never reads memory the game may recycle, and presenting to the screen is the
port's business rather than the game's.

The patch also records how long send_dl actually takes -- worst case and the
count of frames over the 167 ms budget -- because that measurement is what makes
the diagnosis checkable rather than merely plausible.

diff --git a/ultramodern/src/events.cpp b/ultramodern/src/events.cpp
--- a/ultramodern/src/events.cpp
+++ b/ultramodern/src/events.cpp
@@ -284,6 +284,31 @@
                  g_trace_dp.load());
 }
 
+// Combien de temps dure reellement un rendu de liste d'affichage ?
+//
+// C'est la mesure qui decide du diagnostic : le chien de garde de DKR abandonne
+// le RDP au bout de onze retraces, soit 167 ms. Si le rendu depasse ce budget,
+// publier le bord DP a la fin du rendu condamne le jeu, quel que soit l'ordre
+// des messages. On releve le pire cas plutot que la moyenne, parce que c'est un
+// seul depassement qui declenche le chien de garde.
+static std::atomic<unsigned> g_dl_pire_ms{0};
+static std::atomic<unsigned> g_dl_hors_budget{0};
+static std::atomic<unsigned> g_dl_total{0};
+static void trace_dl_duration(std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::time_point debut,
+                              std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::time_point fin) {
+    const unsigned ms = static_cast<unsigned>(
+        std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(fin - debut).count());
+    const unsigned n = g_dl_total.fetch_add(1) + 1;
+    unsigned pire = g_dl_pire_ms.load();
+    while (ms > pire && !g_dl_pire_ms.compare_exchange_weak(pire, ms)) {}
+    // 167 ms = onze retraces a 60 Hz, le seuil exact du chien de garde.
+    if (ms > 167u) { g_dl_hors_budget.fetch_add(1); }
+    if (!trace_sp_enabled()) { return; }
+    if (n % 60u != 0u) { return; }
+    std::fprintf(stderr, "[trace][dl] rendus=%u pire=%u ms hors-budget(>167ms)=%u\n",
+                 n, g_dl_pire_ms.load(), g_dl_hors_budget.load());
+}
+
 void sp_complete() {
     g_trace_sp.fetch_add(1);
     trace_sp("sp", 0);
@@ -416,11 +441,30 @@
                 PTR(u64) displaylist = task_action->task.t.data_ptr;
                 ultramodern::extensions::on_displaylist_submitted(displaylist);
 
+                // Publish the DP edge BEFORE rendering, not after.
+                //
+                // DKR's scheduler carries a watchdog that stock libultra does not
+                // have (__scHandleRetrace, sched.c): it counts retraces while an
+                // RDP task is outstanding, and past ten of them it declares the
+                // RDP hung -- it nulls curRDPTask and resets the DP registers,
+                // WITHOUT clearing OS_SC_NEEDS_RDP on the task. A DP edge landing
+                // after that point walks into __scHandleRDP with curRDPTask == 0
+                // and dereferences null.
+                //
+                // Ten retraces is 167 ms. A software Glide frame on an emulated
+                // Pentium II exceeds that, so tying the DP edge to the end of
+                // send_dl guarantees the watchdog fires eventually.
+                //
+                // Publishing early is safe for exactly the reason sp_complete()
+                // is: the queued task owns an immutable RDRAM snapshot, so the
+                // renderer never reads memory the game may recycle, and the swap
+                // to the screen is ours to schedule rather than the game's.
+                dp_complete();
+
                 [[maybe_unused]] auto renderer_start = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
                 renderer_context->send_dl(&task_action->task, task_action->rdram_snapshot.get());
                 [[maybe_unused]] auto renderer_end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
-
-                dp_complete();
+                trace_dl_duration(renderer_start, renderer_end);
                 // TODO hook the parsed event up to the actual parsing point when a callback is added to RT64.
                 ultramodern::extensions::on_displaylist_parsed(displaylist);
                 ultramodern::extensions::on_displaylist_completed(displaylist);
